# Site Identity and PPID Guardrails

Use these rules when editing auth, IAM, wallet, admin bootstrap, or customer site integration code.

## Canonical identity model

- Treat internal site identifiers (site_*) as ownership and database binding keys.
- Treat normalized hostnames/domains as PPID derivation and runtime credential matching keys.
- Do not assume internal site_* by itself is sufficient for customer site runtime matching.

## PPID derivation requirements

- Derive PPID from wallet secret plus normalized hostname/domain only.
- Normalize host values before use: lowercase, no scheme, no path, no port, strip www. when applicable.
- On customer sites, default identity input should align with window.location.hostname.

## Issuance and storage safety

- Fail closed on issued/requested site binding mismatch.
- When both site_id and site_domain exist, preserve both for validation context.
- Use server-issued canonical site binding when storing issued credentials.
- Do not silently coerce mismatched bindings.

## Credential shape invariants

- Keep admin compatibility: canonical permissionId should remain admin_access.
- Preserve selected admin level separately (for example permission_level).
- Keep timestamps numeric and booleans as booleans.
- Keep scope as an array of strings.

## Deployment and testing expectations

- Prefer minimal additive changes that avoid auth flow rewrites.
- After auth/IAM changes, run smoke validation on live deployment endpoints.
- Do not remove PPID-first and wallet-unlocked requirements.
